// Golden dataset. One JSON object per line; // lines are comments.
// evidence.quote must appear VERBATIM in the source document — the eval
// runner resolves it to a char span and reports any quote it can't find.
// Grow this to 30–50 questions as you expand the corpus.
{"qid": "q001", "question": "What happens when I declare a path operation function with normal def instead of async def?", "expected_answer": "It is run in an external threadpool that is then awaited, instead of being called directly, so it does not block the server.", "evidence": [{"source": "async.md", "quote": "it is run in an external threadpool that is then awaited"}]}
{"qid": "q002", "question": "What does async def do in Python?", "expected_answer": "It declares a function that supports await: Python can pause its execution while awaiting and go do something else in the meantime, then come back.", "evidence": [{"source": "async.md", "quote": "For `await` to work, it has to be inside a function that supports this asynchronicity."}]}
{"qid": "q003", "question": "Can I call an async def function without await?", "expected_answer": "No. An async def function has to be awaited, and await can only be used inside functions defined with async def.", "evidence": [{"source": "async.md", "quote": "When you want to call an `async def` function, you have to \"await\" it."}]}
{"qid": "q004", "question": "What async libraries are FastAPI and Starlette based on?", "expected_answer": "They are based on AnyIO, which makes them compatible with both asyncio and Trio.", "evidence": [{"source": "async.md", "quote": "Starlette (and **FastAPI**) are based on"}]}
{"qid": "q005", "question": "If my database library doesn't support await, how should I declare my path operation functions?", "expected_answer": "Declare them with normal def rather than async def.", "evidence": [{"source": "async.md", "quote": "then declare your *path operation functions* as normally, with just `def`"}]}
{"qid": "q006", "question": "Does FastAPI change how my own utility functions are called?", "expected_answer": "No. Utility functions you call directly behave as written: def functions are called directly (not in a threadpool) and async def functions must be awaited. FastAPI only manages path operation functions and dependencies.", "evidence": [{"source": "async.md", "quote": "Any other utility function that you call directly can be created with normal `def` or `async def` and FastAPI won't affect the way you call it."}]}
{"qid": "q007", "question": "How can a path operation return 201 Created for new items but 200 OK for updates?", "expected_answer": "Return a Response such as JSONResponse directly from the path operation and set the status_code you want on it (e.g. 201 when the item was created).", "evidence": [{"source": "additional-status-codes.md", "quote": "you can do that by returning a `Response` directly, like a `JSONResponse`, and set the additional status code directly."}]}
{"qid": "q008", "question": "Do additional status codes returned via a Response show up in the OpenAPI docs?", "expected_answer": "No. Responses returned directly are not included in the OpenAPI schema because FastAPI can't know beforehand what you will return; you have to document them yourself with Additional Responses.", "evidence": [{"source": "additional-status-codes.md", "quote": "they won't be included in the OpenAPI schema (the API docs), because FastAPI doesn't have a way to know beforehand what you are going to return."}]}
{"qid": "q009", "question": "How can I create a parameterized dependency in FastAPI?", "expected_answer": "Make a class whose instances are callable: declare the parameters in __init__ (FastAPI ignores it) and implement __call__, which FastAPI inspects for parameters and sub-dependencies. Then pass an instance, e.g. Depends(checker), instead of the class.", "evidence": [{"source": "advanced-dependencies.md", "quote": "this `__call__` is what **FastAPI** will use to check for additional parameters and sub-dependencies"}, {"source": "advanced-dependencies.md", "quote": "In this case, **FastAPI** won't ever touch or care about `__init__`, we will use it directly in our code."}]}
{"qid": "q010", "question": "What is the difference between Depends(scope=\"function\") and Depends(scope=\"request\") for dependencies with yield?", "expected_answer": "With scope=\"function\" the exit code after yield runs right after the path operation function finishes, before the response is sent. With scope=\"request\" (the default) the exit code runs after the response is sent. Added in FastAPI 0.121.0.", "evidence": [{"source": "advanced-dependencies.md", "quote": "Using `Depends(scope=\"function\")`, the exit code after `yield` is executed right after the *path operation function* is finished, before the response is sent back to the client."}, {"source": "advanced-dependencies.md", "quote": "And when using `Depends(scope=\"request\")` (the default), the exit code after `yield` is executed after the response is sent."}]}
{"qid": "q011", "question": "Why can't I use TestClient inside async test functions, and what should I use instead?", "expected_answer": "TestClient's internal magic for calling the async app doesn't work inside asynchronous test functions. Use HTTPX's AsyncClient directly (TestClient is based on HTTPX) and await its requests, marking the test with @pytest.mark.anyio.", "evidence": [{"source": "async-tests.md", "quote": "By running our tests asynchronously, we can no longer use the `TestClient` inside our test functions."}, {"source": "async-tests.md", "quote": "The `TestClient` is based on [HTTPX](https://www.python-httpx.org), and luckily, we can use it directly to test the API."}]}
{"qid": "q012", "question": "Does HTTPX AsyncClient trigger my app's lifespan events during tests?", "expected_answer": "No. AsyncClient won't trigger lifespan events; use LifespanManager from asgi-lifespan to trigger them.", "evidence": [{"source": "async-tests.md", "quote": "If your application relies on lifespan events, the `AsyncClient` won't trigger these events."}]}
{"qid": "q013", "question": "Why might updating an item with PUT unintentionally overwrite a stored field?", "expected_answer": "PUT replaces the existing data. If the body omits an already-stored attribute (e.g. tax 20.2), the input model fills it with its default value (tax 10.5) and that default gets saved.", "evidence": [{"source": "body-updates.md", "quote": "because it doesn't include the already stored attribute `\"tax\": 20.2`, the input model would take the default value of `\"tax\": 10.5`."}]}
{"qid": "q014", "question": "How do I apply a partial update to a stored Pydantic model?", "expected_answer": "Dump the input with item.model_dump(exclude_unset=True) to get only the fields actually sent, then create a copy of the stored model with stored_item_model.model_copy(update=update_data), encode it (e.g. jsonable_encoder) and save it.", "evidence": [{"source": "body-updates.md", "quote": "If you want to receive partial updates, it's very useful to use the parameter `exclude_unset` in Pydantic's model's `.model_dump()`."}, {"source": "body-updates.md", "quote": "Now, you can create a copy of the existing model using `.model_copy()`, and pass the `update` parameter with a `dict` containing the data to update."}]}
{"qid": "q015", "question": "How does FastAPI support standard Python dataclasses?", "expected_answer": "Through Pydantic: FastAPI uses Pydantic to convert standard dataclasses into Pydantic dataclasses, so they get the same validation, serialization and documentation as Pydantic models.", "evidence": [{"source": "dataclasses.md", "quote": "FastAPI is using Pydantic to convert those standard dataclasses to Pydantic's own flavor of dataclasses."}]}
{"qid": "q016", "question": "Since which FastAPI version are dataclasses supported?", "expected_answer": "Since FastAPI 0.67.0.", "evidence": [{"source": "dataclasses.md", "quote": "This is available since FastAPI version `0.67.0`."}]}
{"qid": "q017", "question": "How can I avoid regenerating a custom OpenAPI schema on every docs request?", "expected_answer": "Store the generated schema in app.openapi_schema and return it if it's already set, so it is generated only once and reused for later requests.", "evidence": [{"source": "extending-openapi.md", "quote": "You can use the property `.openapi_schema` as a \"cache\", to store your generated schema."}]}
{"qid": "q018", "question": "What are the differences between fastapi dev and fastapi run?", "expected_answer": "fastapi dev runs in development mode with auto-reload enabled and listens on 127.0.0.1 (localhost only). fastapi run is production mode with auto-reload disabled and listens on 0.0.0.0 (all IP addresses).", "evidence": [{"source": "fastapi-cli.md", "quote": "By default, **auto-reload** is enabled, automatically reloading the server when you make changes to your code."}, {"source": "fastapi-cli.md", "quote": "By default, **auto-reload** is disabled. It also listens on the IP address `0.0.0.0`"}]}
{"qid": "q019", "question": "How does fastapi dev set FASTAPI_ENV, and does fastapi run set it?", "expected_answer": "fastapi dev sets FASTAPI_ENV to development before importing the app, unless it's already set (then the existing value is kept). fastapi run leaves FASTAPI_ENV unchanged, so you must set it yourself to detect production.", "evidence": [{"source": "fastapi-cli.md", "quote": "Before importing your app, `fastapi dev` sets the `FASTAPI_ENV` environment variable to `development`."}, {"source": "fastapi-cli.md", "quote": "`fastapi run` currently leaves `FASTAPI_ENV` unchanged"}]}
{"qid": "q020", "question": "What is the recommended way to tell the fastapi command where my app is?", "expected_answer": "Set the entrypoint in pyproject.toml under [tool.fastapi], e.g. entrypoint = \"main:app\". It's preferred over passing a path or --entrypoint each time because other tools like the VS Code extension and FastAPI Cloud can find it.", "evidence": [{"source": "fastapi-cli.md", "quote": "so it is recommended to use the `entrypoint` in `pyproject.toml`."}]}
{"qid": "q021", "question": "How do I deploy a FastAPI app to FastAPI Cloud?", "expected_answer": "Run a single command, fastapi deploy (e.g. uv run fastapi deploy). The CLI detects the app and deploys it, opening the browser to log in if needed.", "evidence": [{"source": "fastapicloud.md", "quote": "The CLI will automatically detect your FastAPI application and deploy it to the cloud."}]}
{"qid": "q022", "question": "How do I serve a single-page app with client-side routing using app.frontend()?", "expected_answer": "Use fallback=\"index.html\" (or the default fallback=\"auto\" when there's no 404.html) so browser navigation requests for unknown paths get index.html. The fallback only applies to GET/HEAD requests that accept text/html; missing assets still return 404.", "evidence": [{"source": "frontend.md", "quote": "For that, use `fallback=\"index.html\"`:"}, {"source": "frontend.md", "quote": "**FastAPI** uses this fallback only for `GET` and `HEAD` requests that explicitly accept HTML"}]}
{"qid": "q023", "question": "What happens if the frontend build directory passed to app.frontend() is missing?", "expected_answer": "With the default check_dir=\"auto\", FastAPI only warns if FASTAPI_ENV is development (which fastapi dev sets); in any other environment it raises an error when the app is created. check_dir=False defers the check until a request is handled.", "evidence": [{"source": "frontend.md", "quote": "When the `FASTAPI_ENV` environment variable is set to `development`, **FastAPI** only shows a warning if the frontend build output directory is missing."}, {"source": "frontend.md", "quote": "In any other environment, **FastAPI** raises an error when the app is created."}]}
{"qid": "q024", "question": "Which tool is recommended for generating a TypeScript client from a FastAPI app?", "expected_answer": "Hey API, e.g. npx @hey-api/openapi-ts -i http://localhost:8000/openapi.json -o src/client.", "evidence": [{"source": "generate-clients.md", "quote": "For **TypeScript clients**, [Hey API](https://heyapi.dev/) is a purpose-built solution"}]}
{"qid": "q025", "question": "Why are generated client method names like createItemItemsPost, and how can I make them cleaner?", "expected_answer": "The generator uses the OpenAPI operation ID, which FastAPI builds from the function name, path and HTTP method to keep it unique. Pass a custom generate_unique_id_function (e.g. tag + function name) to FastAPI to get simpler names.", "evidence": [{"source": "generate-clients.md", "quote": "FastAPI uses the **function name**, the **path**, and the **HTTP method/operation** to generate that operation ID"}, {"source": "generate-clients.md", "quote": "You can then pass that custom function to **FastAPI** as the `generate_unique_id_function` parameter:"}]}
{"qid": "q026", "question": "Should I return or raise an HTTPException?", "expected_answer": "Raise it. It's a Python exception, so raising it (even from a utility function) terminates the request immediately and sends the HTTP error to the client.", "evidence": [{"source": "handling-errors.md", "quote": "Because it's a Python exception, you don't `return` it, you `raise` it."}]}
{"qid": "q027", "question": "Should I register my exception handler for FastAPI's HTTPException or Starlette's?", "expected_answer": "Register it for Starlette's HTTPException, so it also catches exceptions raised by Starlette internals or extensions. FastAPI's HTTPException inherits from Starlette's and only differs in accepting any JSON-able detail.", "evidence": [{"source": "handling-errors.md", "quote": "But when you register an exception handler, you should register it for Starlette's `HTTPException`."}]}
{"qid": "q028", "question": "How can one server with a single IP address serve HTTPS for multiple domains?", "expected_answer": "Using the SNI extension to TLS, with a single component (e.g. a TLS termination proxy) on the public IP that holds all the HTTPS certificates.", "evidence": [{"source": "https.md", "quote": "This SNI extension allows one single server (with a **single IP address**) to have **several HTTPS certificates** and serve **multiple HTTPS domains/applications**."}]}
{"qid": "q029", "question": "How do I make FastAPI CLI trust X-Forwarded headers from a TLS termination proxy?", "expected_answer": "Use the --forwarded-allow-ips CLI option to specify which IPs to trust, e.g. --forwarded-allow-ips=\"*\" if the server only receives traffic from the trusted proxy.", "evidence": [{"source": "https.md", "quote": "If you are using FastAPI CLI, you can use the *CLI Option* `--forwarded-allow-ips` to tell it from which IPs it should trust those *forwarded* headers."}]}
{"qid": "q030", "question": "What type are values read from environment variables in Python?", "expected_answer": "Always str; any type conversion or validation has to be done in code (e.g. with Pydantic Settings).", "evidence": [{"source": "settings.md", "quote": "That means that any value read in Python from an environment variable will be a `str`, and any conversion to a different type or any validation has to be done in code."}]}
{"qid": "q031", "question": "Why use @lru_cache on a get_settings dependency?", "expected_answer": "So the Settings object (and the .env file read) is created only once on the first call and reused for every later request, while still being overridable in tests.", "evidence": [{"source": "settings.md", "quote": "But as we are using the `@lru_cache` decorator on top, the `Settings` object will be created only once, the first time it's called."}]}
